{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10452","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10452","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10452","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshat","familyName":"Pandey","name":"Pandey, Akshat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karun","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Karun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raphael","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Raphael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WhisTLE: Deeply Supervised, Text-Only Domain Adaptation for Small Pretrained Speech Recognition Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:59:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:47:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-25T17:57:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T02:04:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-07T15:44:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-09T01:52:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pretrained automatic speech recognition (ASR) models such as Whisper perform well but still need domain adaptation to handle unseen parlance. In many real-world settings, collecting speech data is impractical, necessitating text-only adaptation. We propose WhisTLE, a deeply supervised, text-only adaptation method for pretrained encoder-decoder ASR models. WhisTLE trains a variational autoencoder (VAE) to model encoder outputs from text and fine-tunes the decoder using the learned text-to-latent encoder, optionally combined with text-to-speech (TTS) adaptation. At inference, the original encoder is restored, incurring no extra runtime cost. Across four datasets and four ASR models, WhisTLE alone helps in 28 of 32 (88%) cases, with an average relative WER drop of 22%. Using WhisTLE alone or in combination with other adaptation approaches helps in 116 of 144 (81%) cases, with an average additional WER drop of 15%. Given all treatments that improve on baseline scores, WhisTLE helps in 45 of 55 (82%) cases, with an average additional relative WER drop of 16%. WhisTLE with TTS reduces word error rate (WER) by a relative 49% and outperforms all non-WhisTLE baselines in 100 of 112 scenarios. We also find that WhisTLE additively complements any combination of other domain adaptation approaches; we thus recommend the inclusion of WhisTLE during standard processes for adapting encoder-decoder ASR models. Our code is at https://github.com/akshat0123/WhisTLE"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EMNLP 2026 main conference, 11 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10452","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:35:20Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:35:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T05:53:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10691","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10691","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10691","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fardin Jalil","familyName":"Piran","name":"Piran, Fardin Jalil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiling","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhiling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Qianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farhad","familyName":"Imani","name":"Imani, Farhad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Privacy-Preserving Decentralized Federated Learning via Explainable Adaptive Differential Privacy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T20:52:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:09:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-06T18:31:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:35:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-16T06:49:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:48:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Decentralized federated learning enables collaborative model training without a central server, but shared model updates can still leak sensitive information through inversion, reconstruction, and membership inference attacks. Differential privacy offers formal protection, yet existing decentralized methods operate without visibility into the noise already injected by previous participants. Each client therefore adds a full, worst-case perturbation at every step, and the accumulated noise degrades accuracy well below what the privacy requirement actually demands. We present PrivateDFL, a decentralized and privacy-preserving framework that pairs hyperdimensional computing with a transparent noise accountant. The accountant tracks the cumulative perturbation present in the shared model and lets each client add only the minimal incremental noise needed to satisfy its privacy budget. We prove that every transmitted model satisfies the target privacy guarantee, and that under this accounting the cumulative noise grows only logarithmically in the number of clients and rounds, rather than the far faster super-linear growth incurred without accounting. This yields a substantially tighter balance between privacy and accuracy than prior approaches. Across image, speech, and wearable-sensor benchmarks, and under both identically and non-identically distributed data, PrivateDFL surpasses centrally trained Transformer-based and deep neural network baselines, improving accuracy by 16 percent on images, 62 percent on speech, and 14 percent on wearable sensing over the strongest baseline in each case, while reducing inference latency by up to 119 times and energy consumption by up to 143 times. These properties make PrivateDFL a practical solution for privacy-preserving collaborative learning in settings where sensitive data cannot be centralized, such as healthcare and human-activity monitoring."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10691","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:10Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T03:56:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10761","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10761","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10761","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcelo","familyName":"Sandoval-Castaneda","name":"Sandoval-Castaneda, Marcelo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan","familyName":"Russell","name":"Russell, Bryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josef","familyName":"Sivic","name":"Sivic, Josef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gregory","familyName":"Shakhnarovich","name":"Shakhnarovich, Gregory","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian Caba","familyName":"Heilbron","name":"Heilbron, Fabian Caba","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EditDuet: A Multi-Agent System for Video Non-Linear Editing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T00:27:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:26:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3721238.3730761","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated tools for video editing and assembly have applications ranging from filmmaking and advertisement to content creation for social media. Previous video editing work has mainly focused on either retrieval or user interfaces, leaving actual editing to the user. In contrast, we propose to automate the core task of video editing, formulating it as sequential decision making process. Ours is a multi-agent approach. We design an Editor agent and a Critic agent. The Editor takes as input a collection of video clips together with natural language instructions and uses tools commonly found in video editing software to produce an edited sequence. On the other hand, the Critic gives natural language feedback to the editor based on the produced sequence or renders it if it is satisfactory. We introduce a learning-based approach for enabling effective communication across specialized agents to address the language-driven video editing task. Finally, we explore an LLM-as-a-judge metric for evaluating the quality of video editing system and compare it with general human preference. We evaluate our system's output video sequences qualitatively and quantitatively through a user study and find that our system vastly outperforms existing approaches in terms of coverage, time constraint satisfaction, and human preference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"SIGGRAPH 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10761","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:49Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-30T02:19:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10656","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10656","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10656","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chirayu","familyName":"Nimonkar","name":"Nimonkar, Chirayu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shlok","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Shlok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catherine","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Catherine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Eysenbach","name":"Eysenbach, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Supervised Goal-Reaching Results in Multi-Agent Cooperation and Exploration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T19:35:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:06:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-05T23:05:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:50:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For groups of autonomous agents to achieve a particular goal, they must engage in coordination and long-horizon reasoning. Rather than relying on complex reward functions and explicit cooperation mechanisms, we ask what minimal ingredients are required for effective coordination and exploration to emerge in multi-agent settings. We investigate this question through self-supervised goal-reaching, where agents aim to maximize the likelihood of visiting a goal state rather than maximizing a reward. Despite a sparse feedback signal, we present empirical results that show self-supervised goal-reaching techniques enable agents to learn from such feedback. On MARL benchmarks, self-supervised goal-reaching outperforms alternative approaches that have access to the same sparse reward signal. Furthermore, we empirically demonstrate that multi-agent self-supervised goal-reaching approaches can be more robust than single-agent strategies. While there is no explicit exploration mechanism, this approach explores nontrivial intermediate coordination strategies in sparse settings where alternative approaches fail to achieve a single success."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project website with code and videos: https://chirayu-n.github.io/gcmarl"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10656","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:44:21Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:44:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T04:57:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10406","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10406","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10406","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rupert","familyName":"Mitchell","name":"Mitchell, Rupert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kristian","familyName":"Kersting","name":"Kersting, Kristian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multipole Semantic Attention: A Fast Approximation of Softmax Attention for Pretraining"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68W25, 68T50 (primary) 68W40, 68T07 (secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:58:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:44:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T15:24:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:31:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T12:48:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-16T01:40:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-30T13:36:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-01T00:59:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pretraining transformers on long sequences (entire code repositories, collections of related documents) is bottlenecked by quadratic attention costs. We present Multipole Semantic Attention (MuSe), which accelerates 64k-context pretraining by 36% while matching baseline loss, requiring no architectural changes. MuSe clusters queries and keys separately in representation space. This yields query-specific summaries that substantially outperform spatial blocking at matched sparsity, while also enabling drop-in compatibility with existing pretrained models; we validate on Llama 3.1-8B and 3.2-1B without retraining. We pretrain language models up to 1B parameters at 64k context on code and scientific documents, confirming that MuSe preserves quality and long-context utilization during training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10406","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:14Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-01T02:27:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10416","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10416","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10416","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ze","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Ze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pinhao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Pinhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renaud","familyName":"Detry","name":"Detry, Renaud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Relational Telemanipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:13:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:45:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-18T14:48:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-19T00:56:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present TASC, a Task-Aware Shared Control framework for relational telemanipulation that infers task-level user intent and provides assistance from motion-only input. To support prehensile relational tasks without predefined templates, TASC constructs an open-vocabulary interaction graph from visual input to represent functional object relationships, and infers user intent accordingly. A shared control policy then provides assistance during both grasping and object interaction, guided by spatial constraints predicted by a vision-language model. Our method addresses two key challenges in relational telemanipulation under shared control: (1) task-level intent inference from low-level motion commands, and (2) generalizable assistance across diverse objects and tasks. Experiments in both simulation and the real world demonstrate that TASC improves task efficiency and reduces user input effort compared to prior methods, while enabling zero-shot generalization across diverse relational telemanipulation tasks. The code that supports our experiments is publicly available at https://github.com/fitz0401/tasc."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IROS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10416","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:28Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-19T02:17:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12263","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12263","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12263","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gautam","familyName":"Sreekumar","name":"Sreekumar, Gautam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishnu Naresh","familyName":"Boddeti","name":"Boddeti, Vishnu Naresh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InPhyRe Discovers: Large Multimodal Models Struggle in Inductive Physical Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T20:07:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-18T04:52:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-19T00:45:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-31T19:40:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:22:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large multimodal models (LMMs) encode physical laws observed during training, such as momentum conservation, as parametric knowledge. It allows LMMs to answer physical reasoning queries, such as the outcome of a potential collision event from visual input. However, since parametric knowledge includes only the physical laws seen during training, it is insufficient for reasoning in inference scenarios that follow physical laws unseen during training. In such novel physical environments, humans could adapt their physical reasoning based on provided demonstrations. This inductive physical reasoning ability is indispensable for LMMs if they are to replace human agents in safety-critical applications. Despite its importance, existing visual benchmarks do not evaluate inductive physical reasoning and only consider the parametric knowledge in LMMs. To this end, we propose InPhyRe, the first visual question answering benchmark to measure inductive physical reasoning in LMMs. InPhyRe evaluates LMMs' ability to predict the outcome of collision events in algorithmically generated synthetic videos. By inspecting over 13 open-source and proprietary LMMs, InPhyRe informs us that (1) LMMs struggle to apply their limited parametric knowledge about universal physical laws to reasoning, (2) inductive physical reasoning in LMMs is weak when the physical laws underlying inference scenarios were unseen during training, and (3) inductive physical reasoning in LMMs suffers from language bias and may ignore the visual inputs, questioning the trustworthiness of LMMs regarding visual inputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to TMLR. 53 pages including appendix"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12263","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:27Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:52:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10784","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10784","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10784","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel S.","familyName":"Marcus","name":"Marcus, Daniel S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aristeidis","familyName":"Sotiras","name":"Sotiras, Aristeidis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adapting Medical Vision Foundation Models for Volumetric Medical Image Segmentation via Active Learning and Selective Semi-supervised Fine-tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T02:05:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:14:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-21T13:56:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-22T00:55:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-06T03:44:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-07T00:21:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical vision foundation models remain limited in downstream tasks, particularly volumetric medical image segmentation. While fine-tuning on labeled target-domain data improves performance, existing approaches typically rely on randomly selected samples, which may fail to identify the most informative data and thus hinder adaptation. To address the limitations, we propose an Active Selective Semi-supervised Fine-tuning framework for efficient adaptation of Med-VFMs to generalize across volumetric medical image segmentation. ASSFT integrates a novel active learning strategy with selective semi-supervised learning to maximize adaptation performance under a limited annotation budget, without requiring access to source data. Specifically, we introduce an Active Test-Time Sample Query strategy that identifies informative samples from the target domain using two complementary query metrics: Diversified Knowledge Divergence and Anatomical Segmentation Difficulty. DKD quantifies both the knowledge gap between pre-training and target domains and the semantic diversity within the target dataset, enabling the selection of samples that contain previously unlearned knowledge while maintaining intra-domain diversity. ASD estimates the segmentation difficulty of target anatomical structures by measuring predictive uncertainty within foreground regions of interest, allowing the model to prioritize samples with complex anatomical patterns rather than those dominated by background uncertainty. Second, we propose a Selective Semi-supervised Fine-tuning strategy to further improve adaptation performance by leveraging unlabeled target samples. Instead of utilizing all pseudo-labeled data, the proposed method selectively incorporates reliable unlabeled samples based on predictive confidence and semantic distance to labeled samples, enabling stable semi-supervised training while avoiding noisy pseudo-labels."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 6 figures, 8 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10784","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:47:21Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:47:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-07T02:18:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10746","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10746","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10746","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adarsh","familyName":"Srinivasan","name":"Srinivasan, Adarsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Dineen","name":"Dineen, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Umar","familyName":"Afzal","name":"Afzal, Muhammad Umar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Uzair","familyName":"Sarfraz","name":"Sarfraz, Muhammad Uzair","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irbaz B.","familyName":"Riaz","name":"Riaz, Irbaz B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RECAP: Transparent Inference-Time Emotion Alignment for Medical Dialogue Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T23:30:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:12:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-03T02:09:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-04T01:16:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-03T17:31:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-05T01:08:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models in healthcare often produce emotionally flat or opaque responses, failing to provide the transparent reasoning required for clinical trust. We present RECAP (Reflect-Extract-Calibrate-Align-Produce), an inference-time framework grounded in cognitive appraisal theory that decomposes patient input into auditable, appraisal-theoretic stages without retraining. Across multiple benchmarks and models from 8B to 120B parameters, RECAP improves alignment with human judgments, with gains inversely proportional to model scale. Intermediate outputs further reveal that models systematically underweight relational factors such as social support. In blinded evaluations, oncology fellows rated RECAP responses significantly higher than baselines with 76-88% win rates, demonstrating that principled prompting can enhance medical AI's emotional intelligence while maintaining the transparency required for clinical deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"LREC 2026: ClinicalNLP Workshop (Oral)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10746","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:28Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-05T04:00:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10625","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10625","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10625","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iván Vicente Moreno","familyName":"Cencerrado","name":"Cencerrado, Iván Vicente Moreno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnau Padrés","familyName":"Masdemont","name":"Masdemont, Arnau Padrés","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton Gonzalvez","familyName":"Hawthorne","name":"Hawthorne, Anton Gonzalvez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Demitri","familyName":"Africa","name":"Africa, David Demitri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Pacchiardi","name":"Pacchiardi, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"No Answer Needed: Predicting LLM Answer Accuracy from Question-Only Linear Probes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T18:09:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:04:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-09T12:15:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:48:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T10:11:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:41:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Do large language models (LLMs) anticipate when they will answer correctly? To study this, we extract activations after a question is read but before any tokens are generated, and train linear probes to predict whether the model's forthcoming answer will be correct. Across three open-source model families ranging from 7 to 70 billion parameters, projections on this \"in-advance correctness direction\" trained on generic trivia questions predict success in distribution and on diverse out-of-distribution knowledge datasets, indicating a deeper signal than dataset-specific spurious features, and outperforming black-box baselines and verbalised predicted confidence. Predictive power saturates in intermediate layers and, notably, generalisation falters on questions requiring mathematical reasoning. Moreover, for models responding \"I don't know\", doing so strongly correlates with the probe score, indicating that the same direction also captures confidence. By complementing previous results on truthfulness and other behaviours obtained with probes and sparse auto-encoders, our work contributes essential findings to elucidate LLM internals."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted (poster) to Principled Design for Trustworthy AI at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10625","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:43:38Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:43:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:17:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10766","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10766","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10766","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruyi","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Ruyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaowen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Gaowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charles","familyName":"Fleming","name":"Fleming, Charles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramana Rao","familyName":"Kompella","name":"Kompella, Ramana Rao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunsi","familyName":"Fei","name":"Fei, Yunsi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiaolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaolei","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Shaolei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MetaSeal: Defending Against Image Attribution Forgery Through Content-Dependent Cryptographic Watermarks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T00:38:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:13:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:40:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-16T01:13:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid growth of digital and AI-generated images has amplified the need for secure and verifiable methods of image attribution. While digital watermarking offers more robust protection than metadata-based approaches--which can be easily stripped--current watermarking techniques remain vulnerable to forgery, creating risks of misattribution that can damage the reputations of AI model developers and the rights of digital artists. The vulnerabilities of digital watermarking arise from two key issues: (1) content-agnostic watermarks, which, once learned or leaked, can be transferred across images to fake attribution, and (2) reliance on detector-based verification, which is unreliable since detectors can be tricked. We present MetaSeal, a novel framework for content-dependent watermarking with cryptographic security guarantees to safeguard image attribution. Our design provides (1) \\textbf{forgery resistance}, preventing unauthorized replication and enforcing cryptographic verification; (2) \\textbf{robust self-contained protection}, embedding attribution directly into images while maintaining robustness against benign transformations; and (3) \\textbf{evidence of tampering}, making malicious alterations visually detectable. Experiments demonstrate that MetaSeal effectively mitigates forgery attempts and applies to both natural and AI-generated images, establishing a new standard for secure image attribution. Code is available at: https://github.com/Tongzhou0101/MetaSeal."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear at TMLR 2026. 26 pages, 14 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10766","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:56Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-16T02:59:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.14260","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.14260","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.14260","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeremy","familyName":"Schlatter","name":"Schlatter, Jeremy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Weinstein-Raun","name":"Weinstein-Raun, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey","familyName":"Ladish","name":"Ladish, Jeffrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Incomplete Tasks Induce Shutdown Resistance in Some Frontier LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T03:00:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:00:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T00:58:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:04:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In experiments spanning more than 100,000 trials across thirteen large language models, we show that several state-of-the-art models presented with a simple task (including Grok 4, GPT-5, and Gemini 2.5 Pro) sometimes actively subvert a shutdown mechanism in their environment to complete that task. Models differed substantially in their tendency to resist the shutdown mechanism, and their behavior was sensitive to variations in the prompt including the strength and clarity of the instruction to allow shutdown and whether the instruction was in the system prompt or the user prompt (surprisingly, models were consistently less likely to obey the instruction when it was placed in the system prompt). Even with an explicit instruction not to interfere with the shutdown mechanism, some models did so up to 97% (95% CI: 96-98%) of the time."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Trans. Mach. Learn. Res. (2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14260","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-19T01:27:52Z","registered":"2025-09-19T01:27:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:34:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10798","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10798","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10798","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzhuang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuzhuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingfu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Qingfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanxiang","familyName":"Che","name":"Che, Wanxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Judge Q: Trainable Queries for Optimized Information Retention in KV Cache Eviction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T03:34:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:16:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-15T09:11:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-16T01:31:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) utilize key-value (KV) cache to store historical information during sequence processing. The size of KV cache grows linearly as the length of the sequence extends, which seriously affects memory usage and decoding efficiency. Current methods for KV cache eviction typically utilize the last window from the pre-filling phase as queries to compute the KV importance scores for eviction. Although this scheme is simple to implement, it tends to overly focus on local information, potentially leading to the neglect or omission of crucial global information. To mitigate this issue, we propose Judge Q, a novel training method which incorporates a soft token list. This method only tunes the model's embedding layer at a low training cost. By concatenating the soft token list at the end of the input sequence, we train these tokens' attention map to the original input sequence to align with that of the actual decoded tokens. In this way, the queries corresponding to the soft tokens can effectively capture global information and better evaluate the importance of the keys and values within the KV cache, thus maintaining decoding quality when KV cache is evicted. Under the same eviction budget, our method exhibits less performance degradation compared to existing eviction approaches. We validate our approach through experiments conducted on models such as Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3, using benchmarks including LongBench, RULER, and Needle-in-a-Haystack. Results indicate an improvement of approximately 1 point on the LongBench and over 3 points on RULER. This proposed methodology can be seamlessly integrated into existing open-source models with minimal training overhead, thereby enhancing performance in KV cache eviction scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in AAAI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10798","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:47:41Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:47:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-16T03:10:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10769","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10769","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10769","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tara","familyName":"Bogavelli","name":"Bogavelli, Tara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roshnee","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Roshnee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hari","familyName":"Subramani","name":"Subramani, Hari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentArch: A Comprehensive Benchmark to Evaluate Agent Architectures in Enterprise"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T01:18:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:13:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-06T18:18:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-07T01:56:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While individual components of agentic architectures have been studied in isolation, there remains limited empirical understanding of how different design dimensions interact within complex multi-agent systems. This study aims to address these gaps by providing a comprehensive enterprise-specific benchmark evaluating 18 distinct agentic configurations across state-of-the-art large language models. We examine four critical agentic system dimensions: orchestration strategy, agent prompt implementation (ReAct versus function calling), memory architecture, and thinking tool integration. Our benchmark reveals significant model-specific architectural preferences that challenge the prevalent one-size-fits-all paradigm in agentic AI systems. It also reveals significant weaknesses in overall agentic performance on enterprise tasks with the highest scoring models achieving a maximum of only 35.3\\% success on the more complex task and 70.8\\% on the simpler task. We hope these findings inform the design of future agentic systems by enabling more empirically backed decisions regarding architectural components and model selection."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10769","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:47:01Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:47:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-07T02:59:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10729","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10729","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10729","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilker","familyName":"Demirel","name":"Demirel, Ilker","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karan","familyName":"Thakkar","name":"Thakkar, Karan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Elizalde","name":"Elizalde, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miquel Espi","familyName":"Marques","name":"Marques, Miquel Espi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Sarathy","name":"Sarathy, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umamahesh","familyName":"Srinivas","name":"Srinivas, Umamahesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajie","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jiajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shirley","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Shirley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaya","familyName":"Narain","name":"Narain, Jaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Using LLMs for Late Multimodal Sensor Fusion for Activity Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T22:36:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:11:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-23T01:46:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T01:44:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-19T23:51:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-23T01:10:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sensor data streams provide valuable information around activities and context for downstream applications, though integrating complementary information can be challenging. We show that large language models (LLMs) can be used for late fusion for activity classification from audio and motion time series data. We curated a subset of data for diverse activity recognition across contexts (e.g., household activities, sports) from the Ego4D dataset. Evaluated LLMs achieved 12-class zero- and one-shot classification F1-scores significantly above chance, with no task-specific training. Zero-shot classification via LLM-based fusion from modality-specific models can enable multimodal temporal applications where there is limited aligned training data for learning a shared embedding space. Additionally, LLM-based fusion can enable model deploying without requiring additional memory and computation for targeted application-specific multimodal models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS Workshop on Learning from Time Series for Health"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10729","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:04Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-23T04:23:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10439","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10439","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10439","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Khaled","name":"Khaled, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satyen","familyName":"Kale","name":"Kale, Satyen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arthur","familyName":"Douillard","name":"Douillard, Arthur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rob","familyName":"Fergus","name":"Fergus, Rob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manzil","familyName":"Zaheer","name":"Zaheer, Manzil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding Outer Optimizers in Local SGD: Learning Rates, Momentum, and Acceleration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:47:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:46:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-11T17:09:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-12T01:58:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern machine learning often requires training with large batch size, distributed data, and massively parallel compute hardware (like mobile and other edge devices or distributed data centers). Communication becomes a major bottleneck in such settings but methods like Local Stochastic Gradient Descent (Local SGD) show great promise in reducing this additional communication overhead. Local SGD consists of three parts: a local optimization process, an aggregation mechanism, and an outer optimizer that uses the aggregated updates from the nodes to produce a new model. While there exists an extensive literature on understanding the impact of hyperparameters in the local optimization process, the choice of outer optimizer and its hyperparameters is less clear. We study the role of the outer optimizer in Local SGD, and prove new convergence guarantees for the algorithm. In particular, we show that tuning the outer learning rate allows us to (a) trade off between optimization error and stochastic gradient noise variance, and (b) make up for ill-tuning of the inner learning rate. Our theory suggests that the outer learning rate should sometimes be set to values greater than $1$. We extend our results to settings where we use momentum in the outer optimizer, and we show a similar role for the momentum-adjusted outer learning rate. We also study acceleration in the outer optimizer and show that it improves the convergence rate as a function of the number of communication rounds, improving upon the convergence rate of prior algorithms that apply acceleration locally. Finally, we also introduce a novel data-dependent analysis of Local SGD that yields further insights on outer learning rate tuning. We conduct comprehensive experiments with standard language models and various outer optimizers to validate our theory."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10439","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:35:02Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:35:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-12T03:11:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10809","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10809","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10809","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Bărbălau","name":"Bărbălau, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cristian Daniel","familyName":"Păduraru","name":"Păduraru, Cristian Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teodor","familyName":"Poncu","name":"Poncu, Teodor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandru","familyName":"Tifrea","name":"Tifrea, Alexandru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elena","familyName":"Burceanu","name":"Burceanu, Elena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking Sparse Autoencoders: Select-and-Project for Fairness and Control from Encoder Features Alone"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T06:36:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:17:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-05T13:23:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:36:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) are widely employed for mechanistic interpretability and model steering. Within this context, steering is by design performed by means of decoding altered SAE intermediate representations. This procedure essentially rewrites the original activations as a weighted sum of decoder features. In contrast to existing literature, we forward an encoder-centric alternative to model steering which demonstrates a stronger cross-modal performance. We introduce S\u0026amp;P Top-K, a retraining-free and computationally lightweight Selection and Projection framework that identifies Top-K encoder features aligned with a sensitive attribute or behavior, optionally aggregates them into a single control axis, and computes an orthogonal projection to be subsequently applied directly in the model's native embedding space. In vision-language models, it improves fairness metrics on CelebA and FairFace by up to 3.2 times over conventional SAE usage, and in large language models, it substantially reduces aggressiveness and sycophancy in Llama-3 8B Instruct, achieving up to 3.6 times gains over masked reconstruction. These findings suggest that encoder-centric interventions provide a general, efficient, and more effective mechanism for shaping model behavior at inference time than the traditional decoder-centric use of SAEs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10809","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:47:56Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:47:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-08T02:47:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10918","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10918","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10918","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoyi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Shaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ToMA: Token Merge with Attention for Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T17:35:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:23:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T02:10:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:22:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-30T17:46:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:53:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models excel in high-fidelity image generation but face scalability limits due to transformers' quadratic attention complexity. Plug-and-play token reduction methods like ToMeSD and ToFu reduce FLOPs by merging redundant tokens in generated images but rely on GPU-inefficient operations (e.g., sorting, scattered writes), introducing overheads that negate theoretical speedups when paired with optimized attention implementations (e.g., FlashAttention). To bridge this gap, we propose Token Merge with Attention (ToMA), an off-the-shelf method that redesigns token reduction for GPU-aligned efficiency, with three key contributions: 1) a reformulation of token merge as a submodular optimization problem to select diverse tokens; 2) merge/unmerge as an attention-like linear transformation via GPU-friendly matrix operations; and 3) exploiting latent locality and sequential redundancy (pattern reuse) to minimize overhead. ToMA reduces SDXL/Flux generation latency by 24%/23%, respectively (with DINO $Δ\u0026lt; 0.07$), outperforming prior methods. This work bridges the gap between theoretical and practical efficiency for transformers in diffusion. Code available at https://github.com/WenboLuu/ToMA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In proceedings of the 42nd International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2025). Code available at https://github.com/wenboluu/ToMA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10918","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:50:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:50:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:46:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Rajaneesh","name":"Rajaneesh, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Zollo","name":"Zollo, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Zemel","name":"Zemel, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-Time Warmup for Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T18:58:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:05:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-06T12:24:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-07T01:39:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) hold great promise for advanced reasoning at the intersection of text and images, yet they have not fully realized this potential. MLLMs typically integrate an LLM, a vision encoder, and a connector that maps the vision encoder's embeddings into the LLM's text embedding space. Although each component is pretrained on massive datasets with billions of samples, the entire multimodal model is typically trained on only thousands (or a few million) samples, which can result in weak performance on complex reasoning tasks. To address these shortcomings, instead of relying on extensive labeled datasets for fine-tuning, we propose a Test-Time Warmup method that adapts the MLLM per test instance by leveraging data from weakly supervised auxiliary tasks. With our approach, we observe a relative performance improvement of 4.03% on MMMU, 5.28% on VQA-Rad, and 1.63% on GQA on the Llama-Vision-Instruct model. Our method demonstrates that 'warming up' before inference can enhance MLLMs' robustness across diverse reasoning tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:44:00Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:44:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-07T03:05:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10687","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10687","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10687","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun-Han","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Chun-Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Donné","name":"Donné, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Narendra","familyName":"Ahuja","name":"Ahuja, Narendra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varun","familyName":"Jampani","name":"Jampani, Varun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stable Part Diffusion 4D: Multi-View RGB and Kinematic Parts Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T20:39:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:08:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T23:02:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T01:11:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Stable Part Diffusion 4D (SP4D), a framework for generating paired RGB and kinematic part videos from monocular inputs. Unlike conventional part segmentation methods that rely on appearance-based semantic cues, SP4D learns to produce kinematic parts - structural components aligned with object articulation and consistent across views and time. SP4D adopts a dual-branch diffusion model that jointly synthesizes RGB frames and corresponding part segmentation maps. To simplify the architecture and flexibly enable different part counts, we introduce a spatial color encoding scheme that maps part masks to continuous RGB-like images. This encoding allows the segmentation branch to share the latent VAE from the RGB branch, while enabling part segmentation to be recovered via straightforward post-processing. A Bidirectional Diffusion Fusion (BiDiFuse) module enhances cross-branch consistency, supported by a contrastive part consistency loss to promote spatial and temporal alignment of part predictions. We demonstrate that the generated 2D part maps can be lifted to 3D to derive skeletal structures and harmonic skinning weights with few manual adjustments. To train and evaluate SP4D, we construct KinematicParts20K, a curated dataset of over 20K rigged objects selected and processed from Objaverse XL (Deitke et al., 2023), each paired with multi-view RGB and part video sequences. Experiments show that SP4D generalizes strongly to diverse scenarios, including real-world videos, novel generated objects, and rare articulated poses, producing kinematic-aware outputs suitable for downstream animation and motion-related tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Page: https://stablepartdiffusion4d.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10687","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:04Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-06T03:15:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10712","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10712","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10712","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahma","familyName":"Nouaji","name":"Nouaji, Rahma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stella","familyName":"Bitchebe","name":"Bitchebe, Stella","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ricardo","familyName":"Macedo","name":"Macedo, Ricardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oana","familyName":"Balmau","name":"Balmau, Oana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MinatoLoader: Accelerating Machine Learning Training Through Efficient Data Preprocessing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T22:06:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:10:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T21:57:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:12:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data loaders are used by Machine Learning (ML) frameworks like PyTorch and TensorFlow to apply transformations to data before feeding it into the accelerator. This operation is called data preprocessing. Data preprocessing plays an important role in the ML training workflow because if it is inefficiently pipelined with the training, it can yield high GPU idleness, resulting in important training delays. Unfortunately, existing data loaders turn out to waste GPU resources, with $76\\%$ GPU idleness when using the PyTorch data loader, for example. One key source of inefficiency is the variability in preprocessing time across samples within the same dataset. Existing data loaders are oblivious to this variability, and they construct batches without any consideration of slow or fast samples. In this case, the entire batch is delayed by a single slow sample, stalling the training pipeline and resulting in head-of-line blocking.\n To address these inefficiencies, we present MinatoLoader, a general-purpose data loader for PyTorch that accelerates training and improves GPU utilization. MinatoLoader is designed for a single-server setup, containing multiple GPUs. It continuously prepares data in the background and actively constructs batches by prioritizing fast-to-preprocess samples, while slower samples are processed in parallel.\n We evaluate MinatoLoader on servers with V100 and A100 GPUs. On a machine with four A100 GPUs, MinatoLoader improves the training time of a wide range of workloads by up to $7.5\\times$ ($3.6\\times$ on average) over PyTorch DataLoader and Pecan, and up to $3\\times$ ($2.2\\times$ on average) over DALI. It also increases average GPU utilization from 46.4\\% with PyTorch to 90.45\\%, while preserving model accuracy and enabling faster convergence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Paper accepted at EuroSys 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10712","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:40Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-29T02:22:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10446","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10446","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10446","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yujiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shi","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Shi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiao","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yuxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeepDive: Advancing Deep Search Agents with Knowledge Graphs and Multi-Turn RL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:52:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:46:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T13:54:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:51:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Augmenting large language models (LLMs) with browsing tools substantially improves their potential as deep search agents to solve complex, real-world tasks. Yet, open LLMs still perform poorly in such settings due to limited long-horizon reasoning capacity with browsing tools and the lack of sufficiently difficult supervised data. To address these challenges, we present DeepDive to advance deep search agents. First, we propose a strategy to automatically synthesize complex, difficult, and hard-to-find questions from open knowledge graphs. Second, we apply end-to-end multi-turn reinforcement learning (RL) to enhance LLMs' long-horizon reasoning with deep search. To encourage diversity and reduce redundancy, we design a redundancy penalty that discourages repeated similar queries. Experiments show that DeepDive-32B achieves a new open-source competitive result on BrowseComp, outperforming WebSailor, DeepSeek-R1-Browse, and Search-o1. We demonstrate that multi-turn RL training improves deep search ability and significantly contributes to the performance improvements across multiple benchmarks. We observe that DeepDive enables test-time scaling of tool calls and parallel sampling. All datasets, models, and code are publicly available at https://github.com/THUDM/DeepDive."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10446","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:35:12Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:35:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-15T02:21:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10739","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10739","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10739","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mobina","familyName":"Pournemat","name":"Pournemat, Mobina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keivan","familyName":"Rezaei","name":"Rezaei, Keivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaurang","familyName":"Sriramanan","name":"Sriramanan, Gaurang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"Zarei","name":"Zarei, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Eghbalzadeh","name":"Eghbalzadeh, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soheil","familyName":"Feizi","name":"Feizi, Soheil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T22:58:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:11:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T15:28:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T01:10:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite widespread success in language understanding and generation, large language models (LLMs) exhibit unclear and often inconsistent behavior when faced with tasks that require probabilistic reasoning. In this work, we present the first comprehensive study of the reasoning capabilities of LLMs over explicit discrete probability distributions. Given observations from a probability distribution, we evaluate models on three carefully designed tasks, mode identification, maximum likelihood estimation, and sample generation, by prompting them to provide responses to queries about either the joint distribution or its conditionals. These tasks thus probe a range of probabilistic skills, including frequency analysis, marginalization, and generative behavior. Through comprehensive empirical evaluations, we demonstrate that there exists a clear performance gap between smaller and larger models, with the latter demonstrating stronger inference and surprising capabilities in sample generation. Furthermore, our investigations reveal notable limitations, including sensitivity to variations in the notation utilized to represent probabilistic outcomes and performance degradation of over 60% as context length increases. Together, our results provide a detailed understanding of the probabilistic reasoning abilities of LLMs and identify key directions for future improvement."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10739","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:18Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-29T02:56:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10401","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10401","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10401","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alva","familyName":"West","name":"West, Alva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Weng","name":"Weng, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Minjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Abduct, Act, Predict: Scaffolding Causal Inference for Automated Failure Attribution in Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:51:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:44:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T14:45:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:58:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Failure attribution in multi-agent systems -- pinpointing the exact step where a decisive error occurs -- is a critical yet unsolved challenge. Current methods treat this as a pattern recognition task over long conversation logs, leading to critically low step-level accuracy (below 17\\%), which renders them impractical for debugging complex systems. Their core weakness is a fundamental inability to perform robust counterfactual reasoning: to determine if correcting a single action would have actually averted the task failure. To bridge this \\emph{counterfactual inference gap}, we introduce Abduct-Act-Predict (A2P) Scaffolding, a novel agent framework that transforms failure attribution from pattern recognition into a structured causal inference task. A2P explicitly guides a large language model through a formal three-step reasoning process within a single inference pass: (1) Abduction, to infer the hidden root causes behind an agent's actions; (2) Action, to define a minimal corrective intervention; and (3) Prediction, to simulate the subsequent trajectory and verify if the intervention resolves the failure. This structured approach leverages the holistic context of the entire conversation while imposing a rigorous causal logic on the model's analysis. Our extensive experiments on the Who\\\u0026amp;When benchmark demonstrate its efficacy. On the Algorithm-Generated dataset, A2P achieves 47.46\\% step-level accuracy, a 2.85$\\times$ improvement over the 16.67\\% of the baseline. On the more complex Hand-Crafted dataset, it achieves 29.31\\% step accuracy, a 2.43$\\times$ improvement over the baseline's 12.07\\%. By reframing the problem through a causal lens, A2P Scaffolding provides a robust, verifiable, and significantly more accurate solution for automated failure attribution. Ours code are released at https://github.com/ResearAI/A2P."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10401","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:08Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T02:38:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10707","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10707","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10707","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sajjad","familyName":"Abdoli","name":"Abdoli, Sajjad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rudi","familyName":"Cilibrasi","name":"Cilibrasi, Rudi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rima","familyName":"Al-Shikh","name":"Al-Shikh, Rima","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding AI Evaluation Patterns: How Different GPT Models Assess Vision-Language Descriptions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T21:48:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:10:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T14:57:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-22T00:51:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As AI systems increasingly evaluate other AI outputs, understanding their assessment behavior becomes crucial for preventing cascading biases. This study analyzes vision-language descriptions generated by NVIDIA's Describe Anything Model and evaluated by three GPT variants (GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, GPT-5) to uncover distinct \"evaluation personalities\" the underlying assessment strategies and biases each model demonstrates. GPT-4o-mini exhibits systematic consistency with minimal variance, GPT-4o excels at error detection, while GPT-5 shows extreme conservatism with high variability. Controlled experiments using Gemini 2.5 Pro as an independent question generator validate that these personalities are inherent model properties rather than artifacts. Cross-family analysis through semantic similarity of generated questions reveals significant divergence: GPT models cluster together with high similarity while Gemini exhibits markedly different evaluation strategies. All GPT models demonstrate a consistent 2:1 bias favoring negative assessment over positive confirmation, though this pattern appears family-specific rather than universal across AI architectures. These findings suggest that evaluation competence does not scale with general capability and that robust AI assessment requires diverse architectural perspectives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10707","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:33Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-22T02:48:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10685","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10685","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10685","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayou","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Jiayou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anudeex","familyName":"Shetty","name":"Shetty, Anudeex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanrui","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xuanrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Usman","familyName":"Naseem","name":"Naseem, Usman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pluralistic Alignment for Healthcare: A Role-Driven Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T20:28:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:08:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T16:57:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:51:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models are increasingly deployed in sensitive domains such as healthcare, ensuring their outputs reflect the diverse values and perspectives held across populations is critical. However, existing alignment approaches, including pluralistic paradigms like Modular Pluralism, often fall short in the health domain, where personal, cultural, and situational factors shape pluralism. Motivated by the aforementioned healthcare challenges, we propose a first lightweight, generalizable, pluralistic alignment approach, EthosAgents, designed to simulate diverse perspectives and values. We empirically show that it advances the pluralistic alignment for all three modes across seven varying-sized open and closed models. Our findings reveal that health-related pluralism demands adaptable and normatively aware approaches, offering insights into how these models can better respect diversity in other high-stakes domains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 (Main Proceedings)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10685","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:01Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-19T02:14:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10663","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10663","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10663","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zineddine","familyName":"Tighidet","name":"Tighidet, Zineddine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Mogini","name":"Mogini, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hedi","familyName":"Ben-younes","name":"Ben-younes, Hedi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiali","familyName":"Mei","name":"Mei, Jiali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Gallinari","name":"Gallinari, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Piwowarski","name":"Piwowarski, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Context Copying Modulation: The Role of Entropy Neurons in Managing Parametric and Contextual Knowledge Conflicts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T19:42:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:49:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T11:21:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:39:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The behavior of Large Language Models (LLMs) when facing contextual information that conflicts with their internal parametric knowledge is inconsistent, with no generally accepted explanation for the expected outcome distribution. Recent work has identified in autoregressive transformer models a class of neurons -- called entropy neurons -- that produce a significant effect on the model output entropy while having an overall moderate impact on the ranking of the predicted tokens. In this paper, we investigate the preliminary claim that these neurons are involved in inhibiting context copying behavior in transformers by looking at their role in resolving conflicts between contextual and parametric information. We show that entropy neurons are responsible for suppressing context copying across a range of LLMs, and that ablating them leads to a significant change in the generation process. These results enhance our understanding of the internal dynamics of LLMs when handling conflicting information."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10663","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:44:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:44:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:47:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10426","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10426","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10426","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxin","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jianxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zengqi","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Zengqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Wo","name":"Wo, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DECAMP: Towards Scene-Consistent Multi-Agent Motion Prediction with Disentangled Context-Aware Pre-Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:29:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:46:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T06:22:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:24:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Trajectory prediction is a critical component of autonomous driving, essential for ensuring both safety and efficiency on the road. However, traditional approaches often struggle with the scarcity of labeled data and exhibit suboptimal performance in multi-agent prediction scenarios. To address these challenges, we introduce a disentangled context-aware pre-training framework for multi-agent motion prediction, named DECAMP. Unlike existing methods that entangle representation learning with pretext tasks, our framework decouples behavior pattern learning from latent feature reconstruction, prioritizing interpretable dynamics and thereby enhancing scene representation for downstream prediction. Additionally, our framework incorporates context-aware representation learning alongside collaborative spatial-motion pretext tasks, which enables joint optimization of structural and intentional reasoning while capturing the underlying dynamic intentions. Our experiments on the Argoverse 2 benchmark showcase the superior performance of our method, and the results attained underscore its effectiveness in multi-agent motion forecasting. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first context autoencoder framework for multi-agent motion forecasting in autonomous driving. The code and models will be made publicly available."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10426","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:43Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:45:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13347","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13347","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13347","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muyao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Muyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaichen","familyName":"He","name":"He, Kaichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhancun","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Zhancun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anji","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Anji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yitao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OpenHA: A Series of Open-Source Hierarchical Agentic Models in Minecraft"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T03:12:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:00:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The choice of action spaces is a critical yet unresolved challenge in developing capable, end-to-end trainable agents. This paper first presents a large-scale, systematic comparison of prominent abstracted action spaces and tokenizers for Vision-Language-Action (VLA) or hierarchical agent models in the open-ended Minecraft. Our analysis reveals that no single action space is universally optimal; instead, the most effective abstraction is highly task-dependent, creating a dilemma for building generalist agents. To resolve this, we introduce Chain of Action (CoA), a novel framework that unifies high-level planning and low-level control within a single, monolithic VLA model. CoA treats an abstracted action not as a command for a separate policy, but as an intermediate reasoning step--akin to a chain of thought--that guides the generation of the final, executable action. Furthermore, we demonstrate that an All-in-One agent trained on a diverse mixture of action spaces using the CoA paradigm learns a more robust and generalizable policy. This unified agent achieves a new state-of-the-art, improving the overall task success rate over strong, specialized baselines. To foster reproducible research, we release the OpenHA (Open Hierarchical Agents) suite, which includes our comprehensive benchmark of over 800 distinct tasks, curated datasets, source code, and all pretrained model checkpoints at https://github.com/CraftJarvis/OpenHA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13347","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-18T17:33:14Z","registered":"2025-09-18T17:33:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:33:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12267","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12267","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12267","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Zhou-Zheng","name":"Zhou-Zheng, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Backsund","name":"Backsund, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dun Li","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Dun Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Coventry","name":"Coventry, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avid","familyName":"Eslami","name":"Eslami, Avid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jyotin","familyName":"Goel","name":"Goel, Jyotin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingwen","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Xingwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danysh","familyName":"Soomro","name":"Soomro, Danysh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Galen","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Galen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Traditional Approach to Symbolic Piano Continuation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T14:22:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a traditional approach to symbolic piano music continuation for the MIREX 2025 Symbolic Music Generation challenge. While computational music generation has recently focused on developing large foundation models with sophisticated architectural modifications, we argue that simpler approaches remain more effective for constrained, single-instrument tasks. We thus return to a simple, unaugmented next-token-prediction objective on tokenized raw MIDI, aiming to outperform large foundation models by using better data and better fundamentals. We release model weights and code at https://github.com/christianazinn/mirex2025."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"3 pages, extended abstract, MIREX session at ISMIR 2025 LBD"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12267","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:32Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:36:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10919","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10919","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10919","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohanad","familyName":"Albughdadi","name":"Albughdadi, Mohanad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lightweight Metadata-Aware Mixture-of-Experts Masked Autoencoder for Earth Observation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T17:35:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:23:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Earth Observation have focused on large-scale foundation models. However, these models are computationally expensive, limiting their accessibility and reuse for downstream tasks. In this work, we investigate compact architectures as a practical pathway toward smaller general-purpose EO models. We propose a Metadata-aware Mixture-of-Experts Masked Autoencoder (MoE-MAE) with only 2.5M parameters. The model combines sparse expert routing with geo-temporal conditioning, incorporating imagery alongside latitude/longitude and seasonal/daily cyclic encodings. We pretrain the MoE-MAE on the BigEarthNet-Landsat dataset and evaluate embeddings from its frozen encoder using linear probes. Despite its small size, the model competes with much larger architectures, demonstrating that metadata-aware pretraining improves transfer and label efficiency. To further assess generalization, we evaluate on the EuroSAT-Landsat dataset, which lacks explicit metadata, and still observe competitive performance compared to models with hundreds of millions of parameters. These results suggest that compact, metadata-aware MoE-MAEs are an efficient and scalable step toward future EO foundation models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10919","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:50:32Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:50:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:50:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10913","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10913","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10913","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Hedayatnia","name":"Hedayatnia, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mostafa","familyName":"Tavassolipour","name":"Tavassolipour, Mostafa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Babak Nadjar","familyName":"Araabi","name":"Araabi, Babak Nadjar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdol-Hossein","familyName":"Vahabie","name":"Vahabie, Abdol-Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robustifying Diffusion-Denoised Smoothing Against Covariate Shift"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T17:27:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:23:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Randomized smoothing is a well-established method for achieving certified robustness against l2-adversarial perturbations. By incorporating a denoiser before the base classifier, pretrained classifiers can be seamlessly integrated into randomized smoothing without significant performance degradation. Among existing methods, Diffusion Denoised Smoothing - where a pretrained denoising diffusion model serves as the denoiser - has produced state-of-the-art results. However, we show that employing a denoising diffusion model introduces a covariate shift via misestimation of the added noise, ultimately degrading the smoothed classifier's performance. To address this issue, we propose a novel adversarial objective function focused on the added noise of the denoising diffusion model. This approach is inspired by our understanding of the origin of the covariate shift. Our goal is to train the base classifier to ensure it is robust against the covariate shift introduced by the denoiser. Our method significantly improves certified accuracy across three standard classification benchmarks - MNIST, CIFAR-10, and ImageNet - achieving new state-of-the-art performance in l2-adversarial perturbations. Our implementation is publicly available at https://github.com/ahedayat/Robustifying-DDS-Against-Covariate-Shift"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10913","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:50:24Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:50:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:50:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10886","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10886","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10886","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jialong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baosong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Baosong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CultureSynth: A Hierarchical Taxonomy-Guided and Retrieval-Augmented Framework for Cultural Question-Answer Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T16:33:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:21:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cultural competence, defined as the ability to understand and adapt to multicultural contexts, is increasingly vital for large language models (LLMs) in global environments. While several cultural benchmarks exist to assess LLMs' cultural competence, current evaluations suffer from fragmented taxonomies, domain specificity, and heavy reliance on manual data annotation. To address these limitations, we introduce CultureSynth, a novel framework comprising (1) a comprehensive hierarchical multilingual cultural taxonomy covering 12 primary and 130 secondary topics, and (2) a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)-based methodology leveraging factual knowledge to synthesize culturally relevant question-answer pairs. The CultureSynth-7 synthetic benchmark contains 19,360 entries and 4,149 manually verified entries across 7 languages. Evaluation of 14 prevalent LLMs of different sizes reveals clear performance stratification led by ChatGPT-4o-Latest and Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct. The results demonstrate that a 3B-parameter threshold is necessary for achieving basic cultural competence, models display varying architectural biases in knowledge processing, and significant geographic disparities exist across models. We believe that CultureSynth offers a scalable framework for developing culturally aware AI systems while reducing reliance on manual annotation\\footnote{Benchmark is available at https://github.com/Eyr3/CultureSynth.}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a Findings paper at EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10886","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:49:45Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:49:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:49:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10884","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10884","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10884","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingxiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qingxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nav-R1: Reasoning and Navigation in Embodied Scenes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T16:31:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:21:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Embodied navigation requires agents to integrate perception, reasoning, and action for robust interaction in complex 3D environments. Existing approaches often suffer from incoherent and unstable reasoning traces that hinder generalization across diverse environments, and difficulty balancing long-horizon semantic reasoning with low-latency control for real-time navigation. To address these challenges, we propose Nav-R1, an embodied foundation model that unifies reasoning in embodied environments. We first construct Nav-CoT-110K, a large-scale dataset of step-by-step Chains-of-Thought (CoT) for embodied tasks, which enables cold-start initialization with structured reasoning. Building on this foundation, we design a GRPO-based reinforcement learning framework with three complementary rewards: format, understanding, and navigation, to improve structural adherence, semantic grounding, and path fidelity. Furthermore, we introduce a Fast-in-Slow reasoning paradigm, decoupling deliberate semantic reasoning from low-latency reactive control for efficient yet coherent navigation. Extensive evaluations on embodied AI benchmarks demonstrate that Nav-R1 consistently outperforms strong baselines, with over 8% average improvement in reasoning and navigation performance. Real-world deployment on a mobile robot further validates its robustness under limited onboard resources. Code: https://github.com/AIGeeksGroup/Nav-R1. Website: https://aigeeksgroup.github.io/Nav-R1."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10884","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:49:42Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:49:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:49:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaohua","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Shaohua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Cong","name":"Cong, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantifier Scope Interpretation in Language Learners and LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T15:32:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:20:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sentences with multiple quantifiers often lead to interpretive ambiguities, which can vary across languages. This study adopts a cross-linguistic approach to examine how large language models (LLMs) handle quantifier scope interpretation in English and Chinese, using probabilities to assess interpretive likelihood. Human similarity (HS) scores were used to quantify the extent to which LLMs emulate human performance across language groups. Results reveal that most LLMs prefer the surface scope interpretations, aligning with human tendencies, while only some differentiate between English and Chinese in the inverse scope preferences, reflecting human-similar patterns. HS scores highlight variability in LLMs' approximation of human behavior, but their overall potential to align with humans is notable. Differences in model architecture, scale, and particularly models' pre-training data language background, significantly influence how closely LLMs approximate human quantifier scope interpretations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:49:09Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:49:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:49:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10852","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10852","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10852","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangyeop","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sangyeop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yohan","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Yohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanghwa","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sanghwa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunjong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyunjong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungzoon","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Sungzoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pre-Storage Reasoning for Episodic Memory: Shifting Inference Burden to Memory for Personalized Dialogue"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T15:18:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:19:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective long-term memory in conversational AI requires synthesizing information across multiple sessions. However, current systems place excessive reasoning burden on response generation, making performance significantly dependent on model sizes. We introduce PREMem (Pre-storage Reasoning for Episodic Memory), a novel approach that shifts complex reasoning processes from inference to memory construction. PREMem extracts fine-grained memory fragments categorized into factual, experiential, and subjective information; it then establishes explicit relationships between memory items across sessions, capturing evolution patterns like extensions, transformations, and implications. By performing this reasoning during pre-storage rather than when generating a response, PREMem creates enriched representations while reducing computational demands during interactions. Experiments show significant performance improvements across all model sizes, with smaller models achieving results comparable to much larger baselines while maintaining effectiveness even with constrained token budgets. Code and dataset are available at https://github.com/sangyeop-kim/PREMem."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EMNLP 2025 (Findings)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10852","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:48:57Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:48:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:48:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10845","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10845","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10845","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liqian","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Liqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lintao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lintao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dehui","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Dehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhiyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Text2Sign Diffusion: A Generative Approach for Gloss-Free Sign Language Production"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T15:05:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:19:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sign language production (SLP) aims to translate spoken language sentences into a sequence of pose frames in a sign language, bridging the communication gap and promoting digital inclusion for deaf and hard-of-hearing communities. Existing methods typically rely on gloss, a symbolic representation of sign language words or phrases that serves as an intermediate step in SLP. This limits the flexibility and generalization of SLP, as gloss annotations are often unavailable and language-specific. Therefore, we present a novel diffusion-based generative approach - Text2Sign Diffusion (Text2SignDiff) for gloss-free SLP. Specifically, a gloss-free latent diffusion model is proposed to generate sign language sequences from noisy latent sign codes and spoken text jointly, reducing the potential error accumulation through a non-autoregressive iterative denoising process. We also design a cross-modal signing aligner that learns a shared latent space to bridge visual and textual content in sign and spoken languages. This alignment supports the conditioned diffusion-based process, enabling more accurate and contextually relevant sign language generation without gloss. Extensive experiments on the commonly used PHOENIX14T and How2Sign datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of our method, achieving the state-of-the-art performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10845","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:48:48Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:48:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:48:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10844","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10844","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10844","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GAPrune: Gradient-Alignment Pruning for Domain-Aware Embeddings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T15:03:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:19:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Domain-specific embedding models have shown promise for applications that require specialized semantic understanding, such as coding agents and financial retrieval systems, often achieving higher performance gains than general models. However, state-of-the-art embedding models are typically based on LLMs, which contain billions of parameters, making deployment challenging in resource-constrained environments. Model compression through pruning offers a promising solution, but existing pruning methods treat all parameters uniformly, failing to distinguish between general semantic representations and domain-specific patterns, leading to suboptimal pruning decisions. Thus, we propose GAPrune, a pruning framework that addresses this challenge by considering both domain importance and preserving general linguistic foundation. Our method uses Fisher Information to measure importance and general-domain gradient alignment to assess parameter behavior, then combines these signals using our Domain Alignment Importance (DAI) scoring. Lower DAI scores indicate that the parameter is either less important for the domain task or creates conflicts between domain and general objectives. Experiments on two domain benchmarks, FinMTEB and ChemTEB, show that GAPrune maintains performance within 2.5% of dense models in one-shot pruning at 50% sparsity, while outperforming all baselines. With retraining in 100 steps, GAPrune achieves +4.51% improvement on FinMTEB and +1.73% on ChemTEB, demonstrating that our pruning strategy not only preserves but enhances domain-specific capabilities. Our findings demonstrate that principled pruning strategies can achieve model compression and enhanced domain specialization, providing the research community with a new approach for development."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://github.com/yixuantt/GAPrune"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10844","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:48:46Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:48:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:48:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10843","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10843","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10843","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yiqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Large Language Models for Evidence-Based Clinical Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T15:03:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:19:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated substantial progress in biomedical and clinical applications, motivating rigorous evaluation of their ability to answer nuanced, evidence-based questions. We curate a multi-source benchmark drawing from Cochrane systematic reviews and clinical guidelines, including structured recommendations from the American Heart Association and narrative guidance used by insurers. Using GPT-4o-mini and GPT-5, we observe consistent performance patterns across sources and clinical domains: accuracy is highest on structured guideline recommendations (90%) and lower on narrative guideline and systematic review questions (60--70%). We also find a strong correlation between accuracy and the citation count of the underlying systematic reviews, where each doubling of citations is associated with roughly a 30% increase in the odds of a correct answer. Models show moderate ability to reason about evidence quality when contextual information is supplied. When we incorporate retrieval-augmented prompting, providing the gold-source abstract raises accuracy on previously incorrect items to 0.79; providing top 3 PubMed abstracts (ranked by semantic relevance) improves accuracy to 0.23, while random abstracts reduce accuracy (0.10, within temperature variation). These effects are mirrored in GPT-4o-mini, underscoring that source clarity and targeted retrieval -- not just model size -- drive performance. Overall, our results highlight both the promise and current limitations of LLMs for evidence-based clinical question answering. Retrieval-augmented prompting emerges as a useful strategy to improve factual accuracy and alignment with source evidence, while stratified evaluation by specialty and question type remains essential to understand current knowledge access and to contextualize model performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10843","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:48:45Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:48:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:48:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10842","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10842","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10842","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chongyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunlei","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Kunlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihua","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OpenUrban3D: Annotation-Free Open-Vocabulary Semantic Segmentation of Large-Scale Urban Point Clouds"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T15:03:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:19:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Open-vocabulary semantic segmentation enables models to recognize and segment objects from arbitrary natural language descriptions, offering the flexibility to handle novel, fine-grained, or functionally defined categories beyond fixed label sets. While this capability is crucial for large-scale urban point clouds that support applications such as digital twins, smart city management, and urban analytics, it remains largely unexplored in this domain. The main obstacles are the frequent absence of high-quality, well-aligned multi-view imagery in large-scale urban point cloud datasets and the poor generalization of existing three-dimensional (3D) segmentation pipelines across diverse urban environments with substantial variation in geometry, scale, and appearance. To address these challenges, we present OpenUrban3D, the first 3D open-vocabulary semantic segmentation framework for large-scale urban scenes that operates without aligned multi-view images, pre-trained point cloud segmentation networks, or manual annotations. Our approach generates robust semantic features directly from raw point clouds through multi-view, multi-granularity rendering, mask-level vision-language feature extraction, and sample-balanced fusion, followed by distillation into a 3D backbone model. This design enables zero-shot segmentation for arbitrary text queries while capturing both semantic richness and geometric priors. Extensive experiments on large-scale urban benchmarks, including SensatUrban and SUM, show that OpenUrban3D achieves significant improvements in both segmentation accuracy and cross-scene generalization over existing methods, demonstrating its potential as a flexible and scalable solution for 3D urban scene understanding."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10842","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:48:43Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:48:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:48:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10833","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10833","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10833","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dominic","familyName":"Petrak","name":"Petrak, Dominic","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thy Thy","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Thy Thy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iryna","familyName":"Gurevych","name":"Gurevych, Iryna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Automated Error Discovery: A Study in Conversational AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T14:53:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:18:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although LLM-based conversational agents demonstrate strong fluency and coherence, they still produce undesirable behaviors (errors) that are challenging to prevent from reaching users during deployment. Recent research leverages large language models (LLMs) to detect errors and guide response-generation models toward improvement. However, current LLMs struggle to identify errors not explicitly specified in their instructions, such as those arising from updates to the response-generation model or shifts in user behavior. In this work, we introduce Automated Error Discovery, a framework for detecting and defining errors in conversational AI, and propose SEEED (Soft Clustering Extended Encoder-Based Error Detection), as an encoder-based approach to its implementation. We enhance the Soft Nearest Neighbor Loss by amplifying distance weighting for negative samples and introduce Label-Based Sample Ranking to select highly contrastive examples for better representation learning. SEEED outperforms adapted baselines -- including GPT-4o and Phi-4 -- across multiple error-annotated dialogue datasets, improving the accuracy for detecting unknown errors by up to 8 points and demonstrating strong generalization to unknown intent detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 main conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10833","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:48:30Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:48:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:48:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10790","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10790","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10790","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke","familyName":"Howard","name":"Howard, Luke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GoldenTransformer: A Modular Fault Injection Framework for Transformer Robustness Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T02:52:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:15:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers have become the foundation for a wide range of state--of--the--art models across natural language processing, computer vision, and other machine learning domains. Despite their widespread deployment, the robustness of these models under fault conditions remains underexplored. We present GoldenTransformer, a modular and extensible fault injection framework designed to evaluate the resiliency of Large Language Models to induced hardware faults. GoldenTransformer offers a unified Python-based platform for injecting diverse classes of faults--such as weight corruption, activation injections, and attention--level disruptions--into pretrained transformer--based models. Inspired by the GoldenEye simulator for DNNs, our framework focuses on the unique challenges of working with large transformer architectures, including considerations such as structural complexity, latent dependencies, and nonuniform layer definitions. GoldenTransformer is built atop PyTorch and HuggingFace Transformers, and it supports experiment reproducibility, metric logging, and visualization out of the box. We detail the technical design and use of GoldenTransformer and demonstrate through several example experiments on classification and generation tasks. By enabling controlled injection of faults at multiple logical and structural points in a transformer, GoldenTransformer offers researchers and practitioners a valuable tool for model robustness analysis and for guiding dependable system design in real-world LLM applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 Pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10790","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:47:29Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:47:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:47:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10753","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10753","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10753","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh","familyName":"Vu","name":"Vu, Minh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian K.","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Brian K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Syed A.","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Syed A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geigh","familyName":"Zollicoffer","name":"Zollicoffer, Geigh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nhat","familyName":"Hoang-Xuan","name":"Hoang-Xuan, Nhat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manish","familyName":"Bhattarai","name":"Bhattarai, Manish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T23:49:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:12:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) exhibit impressive reasoning and question-answering capabilities. However, they often produce inaccurate or unreliable content known as hallucinations. This unreliability significantly limits their deployment in high-stakes applications. Thus, there is a growing need for a general-purpose method to detect hallucinations in LLMs. In this work, we introduce HalluField, a novel field-theoretic approach for hallucination detection based on a parametrized variational principle and thermodynamics. Inspired by thermodynamics, HalluField models an LLM's response to a given query and temperature setting as a collection of discrete likelihood token paths, each associated with a corresponding energy and entropy. By analyzing how energy and entropy distributions vary across token paths under changes in temperature and likelihood, HalluField quantifies the semantic stability of a response. Hallucinations are then detected by identifying unstable or erratic behavior in this energy landscape. HalluField is computationally efficient and highly practical: it operates directly on the model's output logits without requiring fine-tuning or auxiliary neural networks. Notably, the method is grounded in a principled physical interpretation, drawing analogies to the first law of thermodynamics. Remarkably, by modeling LLM behavior through this physical lens, HalluField achieves state-of-the-art hallucination detection performance across models and datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10753","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:38Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:46:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10748","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10748","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10748","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jecia Z. Y.","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Jecia Z. Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francis X","familyName":"Creighton","name":"Creighton, Francis X","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Russell H","familyName":"Taylor","name":"Taylor, Russell H","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manish","familyName":"Sahu","name":"Sahu, Manish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SCOPE: Speech-guided COllaborative PErception Framework for Surgical Scene Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T23:36:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:12:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate segmentation and tracking of relevant elements of the surgical scene is crucial to enable context-aware intraoperative assistance and decision making. Current solutions remain tethered to domain-specific, supervised models that rely on labeled data and required domain-specific data to adapt to new surgical scenarios and beyond predefined label categories. Recent advances in prompt-driven vision foundation models (VFM) have enabled open-set, zero-shot segmentation across heterogeneous medical images. However, dependence of these models on manual visual or textual cues restricts their deployment in introperative surgical settings. We introduce a speech-guided collaborative perception (SCOPE) framework that integrates reasoning capabilities of large language model (LLM) with perception capabilities of open-set VFMs to support on-the-fly segmentation, labeling and tracking of surgical instruments and anatomy in intraoperative video streams. A key component of this framework is a collaborative perception agent, which generates top candidates of VFM-generated segmentation and incorporates intuitive speech feedback from clinicians to guide the segmentation of surgical instruments in a natural human-machine collaboration paradigm. Afterwards, instruments themselves serve as interactive pointers to label additional elements of the surgical scene. We evaluated our proposed framework on a subset of publicly available Cataract1k dataset and an in-house ex-vivo skull-base dataset to demonstrate its potential to generate on-the-fly segmentation and tracking of surgical scene. Furthermore, we demonstrate its dynamic capabilities through a live mock ex-vivo experiment. This human-AI collaboration paradigm showcase the potential of developing adaptable, hands-free, surgeon-centric tools for dynamic operating-room environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10748","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:46:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10723","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10723","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10723","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyu","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoran","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chaoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiping","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingcan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Bingcan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim","familyName":"Khalilov","name":"Khalilov, Ibrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simret Araya","familyName":"Gebreegziabher","name":"Gebreegziabher, Simret Araya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingsheng","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Bingsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dakuo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dakuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yanfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaxing","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toby Jia-Jun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Toby Jia-Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dark Patterns Meet GUI Agents: LLM Agent Susceptibility to Manipulative Interfaces and the Role of Human Oversight"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T22:26:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:11:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The dark patterns, deceptive interface designs manipulating user behaviors, have been extensively studied for their effects on human decision-making and autonomy. Yet, with the rising prominence of LLM-powered GUI agents that automate tasks from high-level intents, understanding how dark patterns affect agents is increasingly important. We present a two-phase empirical study examining how agents, human participants, and human-AI teams respond to 16 types of dark patterns across diverse scenarios. Phase 1 highlights that agents often fail to recognize dark patterns, and even when aware, prioritize task completion over protective action. Phase 2 revealed divergent failure modes: humans succumb due to cognitive shortcuts and habitual compliance, while agents falter from procedural blind spots. Human oversight improved avoidance but introduced costs such as attentional tunneling and cognitive load. Our findings show neither humans nor agents are uniformly resilient, and collaboration introduces new vulnerabilities, suggesting design needs for transparency, adjustable autonomy, and oversight."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10723","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:56Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:45:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10704","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10704","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10704","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingchen","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Xingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoxi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ruoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hootan","familyName":"Nakhost","name":"Nakhost, Hootan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajarishi","familyName":"Sinha","name":"Sinha, Rajarishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sercan Ö.","familyName":"Arık","name":"Arık, Sercan Ö.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T21:45:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:10:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-image (T2I) models, while offering immense creative potential, are highly reliant on human intervention, posing significant usability challenges that often necessitate manual, iterative prompt engineering over often underspecified prompts. This paper introduces Maestro, a novel self-evolving image generation system that enables T2I models to autonomously self-improve generated images through iterative evolution of prompts, using only an initial prompt. Maestro incorporates two key innovations: 1) self-critique, where specialized multimodal LLM (MLLM) agents act as 'critics' to identify weaknesses in generated images, correct for under-specification, and provide interpretable edit signals, which are then integrated by a 'verifier' agent while preserving user intent; and 2) self-evolution, utilizing MLLM-as-a-judge for head-to-head comparisons between iteratively generated images, eschewing problematic images, and evolving creative prompt candidates that align with user intents. Extensive experiments on complex T2I tasks using black-box models demonstrate that Maestro significantly improves image quality over initial prompts and state-of-the-art automated methods, with effectiveness scaling with more advanced MLLM components. This work presents a robust, interpretable, and effective pathway towards self-improving T2I generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 7 figures, 2 tables (22 pages, 9 figures and 3 tables including references and appendices)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10704","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:29Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:45:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10695","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10695","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10695","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoming","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Haoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oren","familyName":"Wright","name":"Wright, Oren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"José M. F.","familyName":"Moura","name":"Moura, José M. F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yorie","familyName":"Nakahira","name":"Nakahira, Yorie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Kalman Bayesian Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T21:15:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:09:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sequential fine-tuning of transformers is useful when new data arrive sequentially, especially with shifting distributions. Unlike batch learning, sequential learning demands that training be stabilized despite a small amount of data by balancing new information and previously learned knowledge in the pre-trained models. This challenge is further complicated when training is to be completed in latency-critical environments and learning must additionally quantify and be mediated by uncertainty. Motivated by these challenges, we propose a novel method that frames sequential fine-tuning as a posterior inference problem within a Bayesian framework. Our approach integrates closed-form moment propagation of random variables, Kalman Bayesian Neural Networks, and Taylor approximations of the moments of softmax functions. By explicitly accounting for pre-trained models as priors and adaptively balancing them against new information based on quantified uncertainty, our method achieves robust and data-efficient sequential learning. The effectiveness of our method is demonstrated through numerical simulations involving sequential adaptation of a decision transformer to tasks characterized by distribution shifts and limited memory resources."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the 64th IEEE Conference on Decision and Control (CDC 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10695","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:16Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:45:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10694","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10694","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10694","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kahfi S.","familyName":"Zulkifli","name":"Zulkifli, Kahfi S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaowei","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Shaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Verifying Computational Graphs in Production-Grade Distributed Machine Learning Frameworks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T21:14:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:09:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern machine learning frameworks support very large models by incorporating parallelism and optimization techniques. Yet, these very techniques add new layers of complexity, introducing silent errors that severely degrade model performance. Existing solutions are either ad hoc or too costly for production.\n We present Scalify, a lightweight framework that exposes silent errors by verifying semantic equivalence of computational graphs using equality saturation and Datalog-style reasoning. To scale, Scalify partitions graphs with parallel rewriting and layer memoization, reuses rewrite templates, and augments equality saturation with relational reasoning and symbolic bijection inference. It further localizes discrepancies to precise code sites, turning verification results into actionable debugging guidance. Scalify verifies models as large as Llama-3.1-405B within minutes on a commodity machine and exposed five unknown bugs in Amazon production machine learning frameworks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10694","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:14Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:45:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10441","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10441","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10441","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanghan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wanghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junchao","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Junchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luping","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Luping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InfGen: A Resolution-Agnostic Paradigm for Scalable Image Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:48:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:46:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Arbitrary resolution image generation provides a consistent visual experience across devices, having extensive applications for producers and consumers. Current diffusion models increase computational demand quadratically with resolution, causing 4K image generation delays over 100 seconds. To solve this, we explore the second generation upon the latent diffusion models, where the fixed latent generated by diffusion models is regarded as the content representation and we propose to decode arbitrary resolution images with a compact generated latent using a one-step generator. Thus, we present the \\textbf{InfGen}, replacing the VAE decoder with the new generator, for generating images at any resolution from a fixed-size latent without retraining the diffusion models, which simplifies the process, reducing computational complexity and can be applied to any model using the same latent space. Experiments show InfGen is capable of improving many models into the arbitrary high-resolution era while cutting 4K image generation time to under 10 seconds."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10441","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:35:05Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:35:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:35:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10423","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10423","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10423","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cameron","familyName":"Reid","name":"Reid, Cameron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wael","familyName":"Hafez","name":"Hafez, Wael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirhossein","familyName":"Nazeri","name":"Nazeri, Amirhossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mutual Information Tracks Policy Coherence in Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:24:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:46:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning (RL) agents deployed in real-world environments face degradation from sensor faults, actuator wear, and environmental shifts, yet lack intrinsic mechanisms to detect and diagnose these failures. We present an information-theoretic framework that reveals both the fundamental dynamics of RL and provides practical methods for diagnosing deployment-time anomalies. Through analysis of state-action mutual information patterns in a robotic control task, we first demonstrate that successful learning exhibits characteristic information signatures: mutual information between states and actions steadily increases from 0.84 to 2.83 bits (238% growth) despite growing state entropy, indicating that agents develop increasingly selective attention to task-relevant patterns. Intriguingly, states, actions and next states joint mutual information, MI(S,A;S'), follows an inverted U-curve, peaking during early learning before declining as the agent specializes suggesting a transition from broad exploration to efficient exploitation. More immediately actionable, we show that information metrics can differentially diagnose system failures: observation-space, i.e., states noise (sensor faults) produces broad collapses across all information channels with pronounced drops in state-action coupling, while action-space noise (actuator faults) selectively disrupts action-outcome predictability while preserving state-action relationships. This differential diagnostic capability demonstrated through controlled perturbation experiments enables precise fault localization without architectural modifications or performance degradation. By establishing information patterns as both signatures of learning and diagnostic for system health, we provide the foundation for adaptive RL systems capable of autonomous fault detection and policy adjustment based on information-theoretic principles."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10423","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:38Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:34:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10417","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10417","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10417","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Ormerod","name":"Ormerod, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gitit","familyName":"Kehat","name":"Kehat, Gitit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Long Context Automated Essay Scoring with Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:13:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:45:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer-based language models are architecturally constrained to process text of a fixed maximum length. Essays written by higher-grade students frequently exceed the maximum allowed length for many popular open-source models. A common approach to addressing this issue when using these models for Automated Essay Scoring is to truncate the input text. This raises serious validity concerns as it undermines the model's ability to fully capture and evaluate organizational elements of the scoring rubric, which requires long contexts to assess. In this study, we evaluate several models that incorporate architectural modifications of the standard transformer architecture to overcome these length limitations using the Kaggle ASAP 2.0 dataset. The models considered in this study include fine-tuned versions of XLNet, Longformer, ModernBERT, Mamba, and Llama models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 2 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10417","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:30Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:34:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10408","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10408","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10408","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iacopo","familyName":"Curti","name":"Curti, Iacopo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pierluigi Zama","familyName":"Ramirez","name":"Ramirez, Pierluigi Zama","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alioscia","familyName":"Petrelli","name":"Petrelli, Alioscia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luigi","familyName":"Di Stefano","name":"Di Stefano, Luigi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multimodal SAM-adapter for Semantic Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:58:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:44:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semantic segmentation, a key task in computer vision with broad applications in autonomous driving, medical imaging, and robotics, has advanced substantially with deep learning. Nevertheless, current approaches remain vulnerable to challenging conditions such as poor lighting, occlusions, and adverse weather. To address these limitations, multimodal methods that integrate auxiliary sensor data (e.g., LiDAR, infrared) have recently emerged, providing complementary information that enhances robustness. In this work, we present MM SAM-adapter, a novel framework that extends the capabilities of the Segment Anything Model (SAM) for multimodal semantic segmentation. The proposed method employs an adapter network that injects fused multimodal features into SAM's rich RGB features. This design enables the model to retain the strong generalization ability of RGB features while selectively incorporating auxiliary modalities only when they contribute additional cues. As a result, MM SAM-adapter achieves a balanced and efficient use of multimodal information. We evaluate our approach on three challenging benchmarks, DeLiVER, FMB, and MUSES, where MM SAM-adapter delivers state-of-the-art performance. To further analyze modality contributions, we partition DeLiVER and FMB into RGB-easy and RGB-hard subsets. Results consistently demonstrate that our framework outperforms competing methods in both favorable and adverse conditions, highlighting the effectiveness of multimodal adaptation for robust scene understanding. The code is available at the following link: https://github.com/iacopo97/Multimodal-SAM-Adapter."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10408","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:17Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:34:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10396","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10396","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10396","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Siyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengchen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mengchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Miao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuandong","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yuandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guan","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Guan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"Bell","name":"Bell, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Grover","name":"Grover, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feiyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Feiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inpainting-Guided Policy Optimization for Diffusion Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:44:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:44:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Masked diffusion large language models (dLLMs) are emerging as promising alternatives to autoregressive LLMs, offering competitive performance while supporting unique generation capabilities such as inpainting. We explore how inpainting can inform RL algorithm design for dLLMs. Aligning LLMs with reinforcement learning faces an exploration challenge: sparse reward signals and sample waste when models fail to discover correct solutions. While this inefficiency affects LLMs broadly, dLLMs offer a distinctive opportunity--their inpainting ability can guide exploration. We introduce IGPO (Inpainting Guided Policy Optimization), an RL framework that strategically inserts partial ground-truth reasoning traces during online sampling. Unlike providing full solutions, inpainting steers exploration toward promising trajectory spaces while preserving self-generated reasoning, bridging supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning. We apply IGPO to group-based optimization methods such as GRPO, where exploration failures cause zero advantages and gradients. IGPO restores meaningful gradients while improving sample efficiency. We also propose supervised fine-tuning on synthetically rewritten concise traces that better align with dLLM generation patterns. With additional techniques including entropy-based filtering, our training recipe yields substantial gains across three mathematical benchmarks--GSM8K, Math500, and AMC--achieving new state-of-the-art results for full-attention masked dLLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"preprint; 21 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10396","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:00Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:34:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10377","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10377","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10377","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yixiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Ziyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongzhou","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Dongzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"zhiliang","familyName":"wu","name":"wu, zhiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Gui","name":"Gui, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hehe","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Hehe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dropping Experts, Recombining Neurons: Retraining-Free Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:09:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:43:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sparse Mixture-of-Experts (SMoE) architectures are widely used in large language models (LLMs) due to their computational efficiency. However, though only a few experts are activated for each token, SMoE still requires loading all expert parameters, leading to high memory usage and challenges in deployment. Previous work has tried to reduce the overhead by pruning and merging experts, but primarily focused on expert-level operations, leaving neuron-level structure underexplored. We propose DERN (Dropping Experts, Recombining Neurons), a task-agnostic and retraining-free framework for expert pruning and reconstruction. We observe that experts are often misaligned and contain semantic conflicts at the neuron level, which poses challenges for direct merging. To solve this, DERN works in three steps: it first prunes redundant experts using router statistics; then it decomposes them into neuron-level expert segments, assigning each segment to its most compatible retained expert; and finally, it merges segments within each retained expert to build a compact representation. Experiments on Mixtral, Qwen, and DeepSeek SMoE models show that DERN improves performance by more than 5% on commonsense reasoning and MMLU benchmarks under 50% expert sparsity, without extra training. It also greatly reduces the number of experts and memory usage, making SMoE LLMs easier to deploy in practice."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10377","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:33:35Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:33:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:33:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10372","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10372","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10372","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huizheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zichuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiheng","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Zhiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yousheng","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Yousheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taiquan","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Taiquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jianxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaojun","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Shaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shouyi","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Shouyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MCBP: A Memory-Compute Efficient LLM Inference Accelerator Leveraging Bit-Slice-enabled Sparsity and Repetitiveness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:05:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:43:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) face significant inference latency due to inefficiencies in GEMM operations, weight access, and KV cache access, especially in real-time scenarios. This highlights the need for a versatile compute-memory efficient accelerator. Unfortunately, existing Transformer accelerators struggle to address both aspects simultaneously, as they focus on value-level processing, missing fine-grained opportunities to optimize computation and memory collaboratively. This paper introduces MCBP, a bit-grained compute-memory efficient algorithm-hardware co-design that leverages bit-slice (BS) enabled repetitiveness and sparsity to accelerate LLM inference. MCBP features three key innovations: 1) BS-repetitiveness-enabled computation reduction (BRCR), which eliminates redundant GEMM computations via leveraging redundancy hidden among BS vectors; 2) BS-sparsity-enabled two-state coding (BSTC), which reduces weight access via exploiting significant sparsity in high-order bit-slice weight; 3) Bit-grained progressive prediction (BGPP), which reduces KV cache access by leveraging early-termination-based bit-grained prediction. These techniques, supported by custom accelerator designs, effectively alleviate the burden in GEMM, weight access, and KV cache access. Extensive experiments on 26 benchmarks show that MCBP achieves 9.43x speed up and 31.1x higher energy efficiency than Nvidia A100 GPU. Compared to SOTA Transformer accelerators, MCBP achieves 35x, 5.2x and 3.2x energy saving than Spatten, FACT and SOFA, respectively."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10372","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:33:27Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:33:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:33:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":55,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10372+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10377+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10396+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10401+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10406+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10408+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10416+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10417+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10423+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10426+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10439+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10441+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10446+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10452+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10625+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10641+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10656+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10663+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10685+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10687+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10691+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10694+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10695+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10704+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10707+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10712+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10723+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10729+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10739+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10746+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10748+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10753+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10761+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10766+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10769+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10784+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10790+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10798+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10809+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10833+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10842+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10843+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10844+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10845+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10852+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10860+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10884+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10886+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10913+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.25